  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly cloudy skies \noverhead as surface high pressure and localized ridging works into \nthe Upper Great Lakes. A few broken streams of lake enhanced / \nconvergent showers are noted on radar and observations in\nMarquette County and may be present across the northern tier of \nthe UP through this evening. Otherwise, temps cool into the \nupper 30s and 40s tonight. Building sfc high pressure keeps \nquiet weather at bay through most of Thursday before potentially\nimpactful weather returns through the weekend. Additional \ndetails below.\n\nThe main concern of this forecast period continues to be the \nevolution of a surface low in the Great Lakes driven by two phasing \ntroughs Thursday night through Friday. Operational deterministic \nmodels and their ensemble counterparts continue to be in good \nagreement that a ~1009 mb surface low will be placed in the vicinity \nof southern Lake Michigan by Thursday evening. Ample northward \nmoisture transport and isentropic ascent ahead of the lead northern \nstream trough will provide enough support for widespread rain \nlifting into the UP Thursday afternoon through Friday morning. \nLatest ensemble guidance continues to increase welcomed rain through \nFriday morning, now suggesting a 75-90% chance for at least 0.5\" \nanywhere in the UP. Early Friday morning through Fri afternoon the \nsurface low feature deepens while departing northeast atop the Lower \nLakes, placing Upper Michigan on the northwestern flank. Strong cold \nair advection and stout pressure rises (upwards of 1-2mb/hr) will \ncontribute to northerly wind speeds reaching 25-35 kts gusting 45-50 \nkts on Lake Superior and the adjacent lakeshores. There is still \nsome uncertainty regarding the maximum gust potential as ensembles \n(NBM and ENS) have decreased the probability of Storm Force gusts in \nthe central lake (20-50% chance) and model soundings don't highlight \nmixing into any 50 kts gust layers. As 850 mb temps cool between \n-5 to -7C, lake effect/enhanced rain on the backside of the system \nwill provide an additional 0.25-0.5\" of precip or the north wind \nsnowbelts and upsloping locations of the west and central high \nterrain once system rain clears the area. Wouldn't surprise me to \nsee some wet snow mix into areas of the interior highlands early \nFriday as freezing levels fall ~1000 ft AGL, though the window for \nany quick show of rain/snow mix is short as soundings show dry air \nmixing into the the DGZ and sub-cloud layer. As winds settle late \nFriday and dry high pressure clears skies from west to east, \ntemperatures will drop like a rock overnight into Saturday morning, \nreaching the mid to low 20s for much if not all of the interior \nwhile some lakeshores may reach freezing for the first time this \nfall. Ridging and dry weather dominate through the rest of the \nweekend with the potential for another warm up / fire weather \npattern Sunday into Monday. Similar to last weekend's fire weather \nsetup, ensembles suggest a 50-75% chance for S to SW winds gusting \nto 35 mph in the typical downslope locations of the west and central \nUP and minimum RHs falling to near 30% in the interior west and \ncentral. Luckily, temperatures are only slated to peak into the 60s. \nFire partners and adjacent parties should continue to monitor the \nforecast as containment and mop up efforts for ongoing wildfires may \nstill be ongoing.",
